===== migration rehearsal ROUND 8 (350 ACCOUNTED POSITION, CACHE, CATALOGUE, PERSISTENCE) 2026-09-24T04:35:44Z =====

WHICH BLOCK DECIDES WHAT, stated before the evidence so the sections
below can be read against it. `CREATE SEQUENCE IF NOT EXISTS` keeps a
pre-existing sequence of ANY shape and reports success, so the file
cannot learn anything from the DDL itself.

  * THE PRE-ADOPTION BLOCK ($m350s$, 350:317-425) DECIDES ADOPTION.
    It runs immediately after the CREATE. Since round 8 it first reads
    the catalogue's own column list against the eight columns it
    declares and the relation's persistence, refusing a difference or
    anything but 'p' by name; then it reads the catalogue row once and
    RAISES on the first attribute that is not the one this file
    configures -- type, increment, MAXVALUE, MINVALUE, CYCLE, CACHE
    (since round 8), the start's range and the start's IDENTITY. A
    sequence it refuses is never adopted: the transaction aborts
    before the ALTER .. OWNED BY, before the CHECK, and before any
    row could draw from it. It judges CONFIGURATION only, and hands
    its reading to 1c.
  * THE POSITION BLOCK ($m350n$, 350:499-628) DECIDES THE REST.
    It runs right after the ALTER .. OWNED BY, whose lock
    (ShareRowExclusiveLock, held to COMMIT) blocks every nextval by
    another session; it refuses unless pg_locks shows this backend
    holding such a lock, re-reads the column list, the persistence and
    the whole shape and requires 1b's reading, reads last_value and
    is_called from the sequence itself, and -- since round 8 -- judges
    the ACCOUNTED value, the next value the sequence hands out once
    this file has committed (the value found, moved by the
    post-check's two draws), against -2^62; and it refuses a next
    value a row already holds. It hands the post-check the policy:
    the floor, the accounted-value query and the value it accounted for.
  * THE POST-CHECK BLOCK ($m350p$, 350:675-859) IS THE SECOND GATE,
    NOT THE FIRST. It exercises what the file produced -- two draws,
    the CHECK against a positive automatic row, a correct one and an
    ordinary player row, and the human sequence's position. Its FIRST
    DRAW must equal 1c's reading; since round 8 it pins the position
    with setval to its second draw, reads it back through the policy's
    own query, and refuses unless that equals 1c's accounted value and
    meets the floor. It holds no c_auto_* constants of its own: the
    attribute decisions belong to the block above, and two copies of
    the same literals are two places for them to disagree.
  * THE RELEASE TRAIN reads `c_floor` and `c_accounted_sql` out of this
    file at the reviewed precursor commit and applies them to each box
    (3g shows it agreeing with the file at the boundary).

The round-5 log said this the other way round at its line 159. The
correction is recorded here rather than in that file, which is
evidence of the round that produced it.
